# These files are written on Windows and published for everyone. Without this, git stores whatever
# the working tree happens to hold -- CRLF -- and the sources arrive with a ^M on every line for
# anyone on Linux or macOS. Settling it before the first commit costs nothing; settling it after
# costs a diff that touches every line of every file and buries the next real change in it.
#
# Scoped to this directory deliberately. The rest of the repository holds .bat files, where CRLF
# is the correct answer and a repo-wide rule would be wrong.
*.py            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
